{%- comment -%}
  One collection at a time, chosen from a list down the side.

  The list is the section's blocks: a block is a collection and, if the
  merchant wants the tab to read differently from the collection's own title,
  a label to call it by. The grid beside it is that collection's products.

  Each tab is a real link to a real collection page, which is what makes the
  section work with the script switched off — and what the script uses when it
  is on, since the link's own URL is what it asks Shopify to re-render the
  grid from. Nothing here has to name a collection twice.

  The `<ul>` the cards sit in belongs to this section and is never replaced;
  only its items are. That is deliberate. The column counts are written onto
  it as the theme's own grid classes, and a fetched grid cannot carry section
  settings with it — see `snippets/all-collections-grid.liquid` for why. Keep
  anything configurable on this element and out of the swap.

  Every card's stylesheet is loaded above rather than by the first card, so
  the markup that arrives by fetch is cards and nothing else.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  comment
    The tab to open with. If the collection this page is actually on is one of
    the tabs — which is the arrangement the template is built for, the "All"
    tab being the collection the template is assigned to — then that is the
    one, and the page opens on the thing its URL says it is. Otherwise the
    first block leads, which is the only other answer that is stable between
    two visits.
  endcomment
  comment
    The page opens on the first tab — "All" — whatever collection the template
    happens to be assigned to. A rail is a way of choosing between collections,
    and the choice it should offer first is the widest one; landing already
    narrowed to one of the tabs makes the other six look like they do nothing.

    A visitor who asked for something narrower says so with `?c=`, and the
    script reads that on the first frame and swaps to it. That is deliberately
    the script's job rather than this file's: Liquid cannot read a query
    parameter, and guessing at one out of `request.url` would be a string match
    that any other parameter could break.

    Only a block that actually names a collection counts. A section just added
    in the editor has an empty first block, and opening the page on nothing is
    not a default, it is a bug with a tidy explanation.
  endcomment
  assign active_index = -1
  for block in section.blocks
    if block.type == 'collection' and block.settings.collection != blank
      assign active_index = forloop.index0
      break
    endif
  endfor

  comment
    Nothing of that type with a collection on it — every tab is a concern, say.
    Then the first thing that names one at all, so the grid still has products.
  endcomment
  if active_index == -1
    for block in section.blocks
      if block.settings.collection != blank
        assign active_index = forloop.index0
        break
      endif
    endfor
  endif

  assign active_collection = blank
  if active_index >= 0
    assign active_collection = section.blocks[active_index].settings.collection
  endif

  comment
    Which group the open concern belongs to, so that group can be drawn already
    open. A dropdown that hides the thing the page is currently showing is worse
    than no dropdown, so this overrides whatever the setting says.
  endcomment
  comment
    What is selected right now, read off the page's own URL. The rail, the
    drawer and the links all draw from these three, so there is one answer to
    "what is showing" rather than three that can disagree.
  endcomment
  assign cur_avail = ''
  if request.url contains 'filter.v.availability=1'
    assign cur_avail = '1'
  elsif request.url contains 'filter.v.availability=0'
    assign cur_avail = '0'
  endif
  assign cur_sort = collection.sort_by | default: collection.default_sort_by

  assign active_group = ''
  assign seen_group = ''
  for block in section.blocks
    if block.type == 'group'
      assign seen_group = block.id
    elsif block.type == 'concern'
      if forloop.index0 == active_index
        assign active_group = seen_group
      endif
    endif
  endfor
-%}

<div
  class="acl{% if section.settings.list_position == 'right' %} acl--reversed{% endif %}{% if section.settings.offset_header %} acl--under-header{% endif %}{% if section.settings.mobile_drawer %} acl--has-drawer{% endif %}"
  data-acl
  data-acl-endpoint="all-collections-grid"
  style="
    --acl-pad-top: {{ section.settings.padding_top }}px;
    --acl-pad-bottom: {{ section.settings.padding_bottom }}px;
    --acl-bg: {{ section.settings.background }};
    --acl-gap: {{ section.settings.column_gap }}px;
    --acl-list-w: {{ section.settings.list_width }}px;
    --acl-item-gap: {{ section.settings.item_gap }}px;
    --acl-list-color: {{ section.settings.list_color }};
    --acl-list-active: {{ section.settings.list_active_color }};
    --acl-sticky-gap: {{ section.settings.sticky_gap }}px;
    --acl-grid-gap-x: {{ section.settings.grid_gap_horizontal }}px;
    --acl-grid-gap-y: {{ section.settings.grid_gap_vertical }}px;
  "
  {{ section.shopify_attributes }}
>
  {%- comment -%}
    Narrow, the rail becomes a drawer. This is what opens it — shown only where
    the drawer exists, so the wide layout never has a button for a panel it does
    not use. Placed before the row so it reads before the products it filters.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if section.settings.mobile_drawer -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The whole band is the control, so the <button> is the band rather than
      something sitting inside one — the tap target is the strip.

      It is not full bleed. The design places it at x=24 in a 402px frame at
      354px wide, which is the theme's own content column with its own gutters,
      so it keeps to `page-width` like everything else on the page rather than
      running to the screen edges.

      The funnel is the design's own path rather than something drawn to look
      like it — a funnel is a glyph, not a rectangle, and the outline here is
      the one the file exports.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <div class="page-width acl__filterbar-wrap">
      <button class="acl__filterbar" type="button" data-acl-open>
      <svg class="acl__filter-icon" viewBox="0 0 10.2 11.2" width="10" height="11" fill="none" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
